 "text": "Two retirees, same $1 million, the same $60 ,000 a year to live on. One of them can run out of money, the other never will. Here's the difference. Meet James. He puts all of it into the S &P 500 fund, VOO, and takes out 6 % a year. James retires right into a rough market, down 15%, then down 10, then down 5. But his bills don't stop, so he needs to keep selling even at the bottom. In three years, his million is down to $575 ,000. meet Carol, same bad start, but this time she buys income first. She puts $500 ,000 into a private pension account paying 6 .5%. That's $32 ,500 every year for the rest of her life, no matter what the market does. But if the markets were to average a little less, about 6 .6%, James runs out of money completely. In year 26, Carol, she still has money invested, and her $32 ,500 shows up every year for the rest of her life. James is hoping that the market cooperates. Carol has a paycheck.",
